{"type":"message","id":"df03d6f6","parentId":"9eba5ec2","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:24:11.600Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_Fx4aVr3xizRiBsxzZruuhd6L","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nDB_PATH=\"/home/bhard/.openclaw/article_history.db\"\nACTIVE_URL_FILE=\"/tmp/openclaw_active_url.txt\"\n\n# Initialize DB\nsqlite3 \"$DB_PATH\" \"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS history (url TEXT PRIMARY KEY, timestamp DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\"\n\n# Clean up older than 7 days automatically\nsqlite3 \"$DB_PATH\" \"DELETE FROM history WHERE timestamp <= datetime('now', '-7 days');\"\n\ncase \"$1\" in\n    check)\n        URL=$2\n        SAFE_URL=$(echo \"$URL\" | sed \"s/'/''/g\")\n        # EXACT match verification\n        COUNT=$(sqlite3 \"$DB_PATH\" \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM history WHERE url = '${SAFE_URL}';\")\n        if [ \"$COUNT\" -gt 0 ]; then\n            echo \"EXISTS\"\n        else\n            # Save it to the side-channel file so WP-Publisher can find it later!\n            echo \"$URL\" > \"$ACTIVE_URL_FILE\"\n            echo \"NOT_FOUND\"\n        fi\n        ;;\n    add)\n        URL=$2\n        CLEAN_URL=$(echo \"$URL\" | sed \"s/'/''/g\")\n        if [ -n \"$CLEAN_URL\" ]; then\n            sqlite3 \"$DB_PATH\" \"INSERT OR IGNORE INTO history (url) VALUES ('${CLEAN_URL}');\"\n        fi\n        echo \"ADDED\"\n        ;;\n    *)\n        echo \"Usage: $0 check <url> | add <url>\"\n        ;;\nesac\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779539051599}}
{"type":"message","id":"cdb048a7","parentId":"df03d6f6","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:24:11.600Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_twk9VrKgJYFeyHlCPBriIYUP","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"congress-polymarket-kalshi-insider-trading-probe-2026-05-23\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US congressional insider-trading probe into prediction markets Polymarket and Kalshi\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"prediction market probe\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Congress opens insider-trading probe into Polymarket and Kalshi over fears government officials could profit from nonpublic information\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer said the committee is investigating Polymarket and Kalshi over whether government employees may be using insider or classified information to profit from prediction-market trades tied to policy, elections, wars, and other state actions.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour seeking records on identity verification, geographic restrictions, and how the platforms detect and flag anomalous trading activity.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Comer referenced suspiciously timed trades highlighted in a May 13 New York Times report, including bets linked to Israel's military actions against Iran, a Trump ceasefire announcement, and congressional election contracts.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph added that prosecutors previously charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke with using confidential government information to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket contracts tied to the capture of Nicolás Maduro; Van Dyke pleaded not guilty.\",\n    \"CoinDesk cited Bubblemaps CEO Nicolas Vaiman saying his team identified 80 Polymarket bets with a 98% win rate, a pattern he argued is statistically impossible to explain by luck and therefore suggestive of information asymmetry or insider trading.\",\n    \"Decrypt reported that Kalshi launched Americans for Fair Markets, a well-capitalized advocacy group tied to former White House communications aide Taylor Budowich, on the same day the probe became public, underscoring how high the policy stakes have become for prediction markets in Washington.\",\n    \"CoinDesk said Bernstein estimated prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could hit about $240 billion in 2026, and may grow toward $1 trillion by 2030, explaining why lawmakers now see the sector as large enough to create national-security, ethics, and consumer-protection risks.\",\n    \"Across the coverage, both companies said they maintain anti-insider-trading safeguards and will engage with lawmakers, while the probe could lead to legislation restricting or banning participation by members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\\n\\nCoinDesk reported that the U.S. House Oversight Committee is opening a probe into prediction-market platforms Polymarket and Kalshi amid fears that government employees could use insider or classified information to make money on contracts tied to public policy, elections, and geopolitical events. The article said Chair James Comer told CNBC that members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees may have access to information that could generate “huge profits” when traded through prediction markets. CoinDesk said Comer plans not only to investigate how widespread such conduct may already be, but also to build a case for legislation that could bar government officials from participating in these markets entirely.\\n\\nThe piece said Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour requesting internal records and clarity on a set of core compliance controls. Those requests focus on identity verification, enforcement of geographic restrictions, and the detection of anomalous trading activity. CoinDesk framed the probe as part of a broader congressional effort to understand whether these platforms can be manipulated by actors with privileged access to national-security or policy information.\\n\\nCoinDesk also gave important market context. It cited a Bernstein report from April estimating that prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could rise to around $240 billion in 2026, and may approach $1 trillion by 2030 as the category expands from niche wagering into broader “information markets” spanning sports, politics, crypto, and macroeconomic events. The article further quoted Bubblemaps co-founder and CEO Nicolas Vaiman, who warned that visible irregular betting patterns could create national-security concerns if hostile foreign actors can infer privileged knowledge from public market activity. Vaiman said his team found 80 bets on Polymarket with a 98% win rate, arguing that such results are statistically implausible through luck alone.\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinTelegraph\\nURL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\n\\nCoinTelegraph confirmed that the investigation centers on allegedly suspiciously timed trades involving government-sensitive events. The outlet said Comer was referring in part to a May 13 New York Times report describing users who bet on Israel’s military actions against Iran, on President Donald Trump announcing a ceasefire in that conflict, and on contracts connected to congressional elections. According to CoinTelegraph, Comer’s public comments suggest lawmakers believe prediction markets may offer a new venue where politically connected or government-linked insiders can monetize nonpublic information faster than in traditional financial markets.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph added legally significant background by pointing to a prior Justice Department case announced in April. Prosecutors charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke over allegations that he used confidential government information related to the operation targeting Nicolás Maduro to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket event contracts. The report said Van Dyke pleaded not guilty and was released on $250,000 bail with travel restrictions. That case matters because it gives Congress a concrete enforcement example rather than a purely theoretical risk.\\n\\nThe article also noted that Polymarket said in March it had updated its approach to potential insider trading, while Kalshi said in April that it had barred three U.S. politicians from betting on their own races. Both firms indicated they would engage with lawmakers and defended their integrity controls. CoinTelegraph’s framing makes clear that the present probe is not happening in isolation: it follows a week of intensifying scrutiny in Washington and escalating concern that event contracts may blur the boundary between financial markets, gambling, and ethics law.\\n\\nSOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\\n\\nDecrypt approached the story through Kalshi’s policy response. It reported that Kalshi unveiled Americans for Fair Markets, described as a “well-capitalized” advocacy group, the same day Congress opened the insider-trading probe. The group tapped Taylor Budowich, a former White House communications official and pro-Trump PAC leader, as a strategic adviser. Decrypt said the launch is designed to shape how Capitol Hill views prediction markets and to counter attacks from entrenched sportsbook and casino interests.\\n\\nDecrypt stressed that Kalshi is trying to distinguish itself from Polymarket and from traditional betting operators by emphasizing its status as a federally regulated exchange under the CFTC. The article quoted rhetoric from Americans for Fair Markets arguing that banning prediction markets would simply push activity offshore to venues with weaker identity checks, lighter consumer protections, and less enforcement against insider trading. At the same time, Decrypt showed that the gaming lobby is fighting back, with American Gaming Association CEO Bill Miller telling Congress that these platforms are essentially backdoor sports-betting operations wrapped in financial-market language.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nAcross all three reports, the strongest single event is not merely that Congress asked a few questions, but that U.S. lawmakers have escalated prediction markets into a national policy issue touching ethics, market integrity, gambling law, and national security. The probe matters because Polymarket and Kalshi sit at the center of a rapidly expanding sector whose contracts can reflect military action, diplomatic announcements, elections, and government decisions in real time. If officials with privileged access are trading in that environment, the issue is larger than ordinary platform compliance; it becomes a state-integrity problem.\\n\\nThe coverage also reveals why this story has momentum right now. CoinDesk provides scale, showing prediction markets are becoming large enough to matter systemically. CoinTelegraph provides the enforcement trail and the concrete Maduro-related case that makes the risk legible to lawmakers. Decrypt shows the immediate political counteroffensive, with Kalshi moving to build a lobbying apparatus as scrutiny intensifies. Taken together, the reporting suggests Washington is entering a new phase: prediction markets are no longer just an innovation story or a crypto-curiosity story, but a contested regulatory battleground that could soon produce new rules on who may trade, what event contracts are permissible, and how platforms must police insider information.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779539051600}}
{"type":"message","id":"ec787963","parentId":"cdb048a7","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:24:20.540Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cqnv0lLvNmBeCsdw4RpuZHwb","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport requests, sys, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\nfrom email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone, timedelta\nfeeds = {\n    'CoinDesk':'https://www.coindesk.com/arc/outboundfeeds/rss/',\n    'CoinTelegraph':'https://cointelegraph.com/rss',\n    'Decrypt':'https://decrypt.co/feed',\n    'GoogleNews':'https://news.google.com/rss/search?q=cryptocurrency+OR+blockchain+OR+ethereum+OR+solana+OR+altcoin+OR+%22digital+assets%22+OR+XRP+OR+cardano+-dogecoin+-shiba+-pepe+-floki+-bonk+-wif+-memecoin+-%22meme+coin%22&hl=en-US&gl=US&ceid=US:en',\n    'BeInCrypto':'https://beincrypto.com/feed/',\n    'TheBlock':'https://www.theblock.co/rss.xml',\n    'CryptoSlate':'https://cryptoslate.com/feed/',\n    'CryptoFoxMarkets':'https://cryptofox.news/rss/markets/',\n    'CryptoFoxReg':'https://cryptofox.news/rss/regulation/',\n}\nua='Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; OpenClawScout/1.0)'\nnow=datetime.now(timezone.utc)\ncutoff=now-timedelta(hours=24)\nfor name,url in feeds.items():\n    print('\\n###',name,url)\n    try:\n        r=requests.get(url,headers={'User-Agent':ua},timeout=20)\n        print('status',r.status_code,'len',len(r.text))\n        text=r.text\n        if r.status_code!=200 or not text.strip():\n            continue\n        root=ET.fromstring(text)\n        items=[]\n        for item in root.findall('.//item')[:20]:\n            title=(item.findtext('title') or '').strip().replace('\\n',' ')\n            link=(item.findtext('link') or '').strip()\n            pub=item.findtext('pubDate') or item.findtext('{http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/}date') or ''\n            dt=None\n            if pub:\n                try: dt=parsedate_to_datetime(pub)\n                except Exception:\n                    try: dt=datetime.fromisoformat(pub.replace('Z','+00:00'))\n                    except Exception: pass\n            if dt and dt.tzinfo is None: dt=dt.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)\n            fresh = (dt and dt>=cutoff)\n            items.append((fresh,dt,title,link))\n        fresh_items=[x for x in items if x[0]]\n        print('fresh',len(fresh_items),'of',len(items))\n        for fresh,dt,title,link in fresh_items[:10]:\n            print('-', dt.isoformat() if dt else '?', '|', title)\n            print(' ', link)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print('ERR',e)\nPY","workdir":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-researcher","yieldMs":1000,"timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":3870,"output":902,"cacheRead":15104,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":19876,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779539051601,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DifgjGTw6Z8aotpElM0Hcg0osTMm1"}}
